WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.035
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-Award-winning actor

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:06.406
you know best from his roles
in shows such as "Boston Legal,"

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:08.875
"The Office," "The Practice,"
and, of course, "The Blacklist."

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:11.044
The 200th episode
of "The Blacklist"

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.713
airs this Sunday, March 19th,
here on NBC.

00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:15.114
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:18.785
-Why are you in my home
at this hour? This is not okay.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:21.387
-You're right. It's not.
I understand that.

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:23.456
And under any other
circumstances,

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:25.291
I wouldn't have imposed.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:26.759
-Did you sleep here?

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:30.563
-A very quick catnap,
just on the bench over there.

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.432
Truly a half-hour at most.

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:35.201
And then the time it took
to put together breakfast.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:39.372
You see, I was at Mount Vernon,
playing a very small part

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:41.607
in a Revolutionary War
reenactment

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:43.409
when I got to drinking
quite late

00:00:43.409 --> 00:00:45.611
with the 1st Delaware Regiment.

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:47.647
One thing led to another.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:51.618
Those Blue Hen boys really know
how to hold their whiskey.

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:53.653
-Please welcome back
to the show our friend

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:55.188
James Spader, everybody!

00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:57.890
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:25.852
♪♪

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:29.755
That's James Spader.
Guys, it's very --

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:32.558
It's very rare to get
exciting news from the guest

00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:34.794
while you walk
from there to here.

00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:37.763
But you just whispered to me
some exciting news.

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:40.500
-I did. No, I saw Flaco live
in the park.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.370
I -- I was walking one day,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.674
and there were
all these people crowded

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:51.076
around the base of this tree.

00:01:51.076 --> 00:01:54.781
And then also lenses like this.

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:56.382
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:58.351
And they'd been parked there,
obviously, for some time.

00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:01.821
And he was up in the tree.
And I saw him up there.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:03.723
I will say this, though.

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:06.426
You know, I mean,
I don't want to,

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:08.761
you know, send any shade...

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.263
-Right.
Is this going to be anti-Flaco?

00:02:11.263 --> 00:02:12.899
-...on your bet. No, no.

00:02:12.899 --> 00:02:14.634
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.603
no. I just --

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:19.605
I -- Really.
I'm, you know, in my --

00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:25.044
I'm 63, and just this year
for the first time,

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:27.580
I heard, in multiple --
from multiple sources,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.648
like, reading it in all --

00:02:29.648 --> 00:02:32.919
in several different articles
just this year,

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.655
including an article
about Flaco,

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:39.158
that owls of the bird species

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:41.394
are actually incredibly stupid.

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:42.828
-Alright.

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:44.730
-So that expression...
-Okay.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.666
-...of Flaco, who is like...
-Yeah.

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:50.303
You're saying
that maybe he wasn't

00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:52.371
like, "How dare you guys,"
and that's more just how --

00:02:52.371 --> 00:02:53.772
That's how he looks
all the time?

00:02:53.772 --> 00:02:55.441
-He certainly --
He certainly wasn't like,

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:57.143
"What, do you think
I'm an idiot?"

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:58.478
No, it wasn't that.
-Okay.

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:00.412
-It was like, "Yeah?"
-Well, thanks for --

00:03:00.412 --> 00:03:02.281
Thanks for settin' us
straight, James.

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:04.750
[ Laughter ]

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.719
I like that, like,
all of a sudden, what are you --

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.254
what are you --
Did you just start

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.590
reading bird books at 63
that you're like --

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.958
"Now I can't stop reading
how dumb owls are."

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:16.095
-Yeah, I really --
I don't know how it came about,

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:20.266
but it's amazing
how there's been such, you know,

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:25.638
a dichotomous narrative about
owls, like, particularly --

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:27.674
I mean, even the little pictures
you showed

00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:29.676
is like the owl as a teacher.
-Yeah.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:31.744
-And, you know,
it must be their sort of

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:32.845
furry eyebrows or something.

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:34.247
-Yeah,
but when you think about it,

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:36.716
I mean, obviously,
everybody I know --

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:39.385
every person I knew who slept
all day and was up all night,

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:42.187
they were dummies, too, so --
-Yes.

00:03:42.187 --> 00:03:44.290
Amazing hearing.
-Yeah.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.059
They -- It's very nice for you
to end this on an up note.

00:03:47.059 --> 00:03:48.761
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.761 --> 00:03:50.963
-I mean, they don't understand
a thing they hear.

00:03:50.963 --> 00:03:52.465
-Just doing my part.

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:54.199
-So, 200 episodes.

00:03:54.199 --> 00:03:56.235
This is the final season
of this incredible show.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:57.770
Congratulations.
-Thank you. Thank you.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.005
[ Cheers and applause ]
-And -- I mean,

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:03.142
shows don't run
like this anymore.

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:05.010
You received a --
This is lovely.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.180
There was --
You know, sometimes I think

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:08.681
people might not know this.

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:10.683
When you hit a milestone,
the show will give out

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.619
sort of a crew gift
to everybody.

00:04:12.619 --> 00:04:16.455
-Yes, I mean, there's enormous
amount of gift-giving, you know,

00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:19.058
around the corporate culture
of all kinds, you know.

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:21.561
-Yeah.
-But -- And on sets, too.

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:23.829
You know,
and it's a very strange thing,

00:04:23.829 --> 00:04:25.964
I think, in that --
You know, every --

00:04:25.964 --> 00:04:30.536
everybody earns a good living
on our show, you know?

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:33.539
And -- But, like,
we'll give the cast,

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:35.941
and the producers will give
the whole crew,

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:37.877
you know, a Christmas gift.
-Yeah.

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:40.646
-This year, it was
an electronically heated vest.

00:04:40.646 --> 00:04:41.981
-Very nice. Very thoughtful.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:44.249
-But, you know,
we all know each other

00:04:44.249 --> 00:04:45.751
really well, you know?

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:47.987
We all work together every day
and so on and so forth.

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:49.722
So it's a different thing.

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:54.493
You know, so whoever it is
who's in charge of, you know,

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:57.363
picking out corporate gifts
from the studio

00:04:57.363 --> 00:05:01.667
or the network
to the cast members or the crew,

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:04.270
you know, they don't
really know anybody at all.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:05.772
-Yeah.

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:07.506
-And they're getting presents
for people that,

00:05:07.506 --> 00:05:12.445
as I said, you know,
can afford their things.

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:14.680
The only person who probably
can't very well

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:18.084
is the person who's
having to decide on that gift.

00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:19.552
-Right.
-You know, that's probably --

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:21.153
-The one person who can
actually use the gift, right.

00:05:21.153 --> 00:05:24.089
-The one person who could
really use the gift, you know.

00:05:24.089 --> 00:05:26.592
Anyway -- [ Laughs ]

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:29.628
I remember I did a show

00:05:29.628 --> 00:05:34.566
for some time for ABC

00:05:34.566 --> 00:05:36.936
called "Boston Legal."
-Of course.

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:38.637
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:38.637 --> 00:05:40.840
Of course.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.109
-And, you know, it was
a fairly successful show.

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:46.446
It won a whole bunch of Emmys,
and, you know, it did very well.

00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:49.648
And I remember once,
sort of late in the game,

00:05:49.648 --> 00:05:55.488
I got a gift at one point
from ABC,

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:59.491
and it was a Tiffany box
from Tiffany's store.

00:05:59.491 --> 00:06:00.726
And I was, "Oh, great.

00:06:00.726 --> 00:06:02.128
This --
What's this going to be?"

00:06:02.128 --> 00:06:05.198
And I open it up,
and it was a bracelet.

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:07.133
[ Laughter ]

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:14.306
-Yeah.
-A silver cuff.

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:15.875
-Yeah.
-Bracelet.

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:18.577
-And did you immediately think,
"I'm never going to wear this"?

00:06:18.577 --> 00:06:19.978
-Well --

00:06:19.978 --> 00:06:21.714
I mean, I didn't really
even know what to do with it...

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:22.982
-Yeah.
-...let alone wear it.

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:25.751
But -- Anyway.
Yeah, so this year --

00:06:25.751 --> 00:06:29.121
Well, for the 100th episode,

00:06:29.121 --> 00:06:33.426
our entire cast and crew
got TVs.

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:34.827
-Alright. Well, that's --
that's very nice.

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:36.728
-Very nice.
-Yeah.

00:06:36.728 --> 00:06:40.999
-I mean, they were small
black-and-whites with antennas.

00:06:40.999 --> 00:06:42.568
[ Laughter ]
-Sure.

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.403
-A separate VHF and UHF.
-Of course.

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:46.472
Yeah, well, I mean,
they've got to get it

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:47.839
for a bunch of people, you know?

00:06:47.839 --> 00:06:50.343
-A TV nonetheless.
-Yeah.

00:06:50.343 --> 00:06:51.777
-No. We got really nice TVs.

00:06:51.777 --> 00:06:53.912
-And that's a, you know,
thoughtful gift.

00:06:53.912 --> 00:06:55.214
Everybody needs one.

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:56.648
-You know, that was
the 100th episode.

00:06:56.648 --> 00:06:59.318
So then, now think like
a hundred episodes later,

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:03.389
so that's several seasons,
right?

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:07.827
So, my gift arrived in my
dressing room, but it was...

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:13.466
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:15.734
I always think of the --
You know, I'm --

00:07:15.734 --> 00:07:17.236
I'm --
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:19.504
I earn a very respectable
salary, you know?

00:07:19.504 --> 00:07:21.641
And I feel like,
you know, you --

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:23.509
The salary that you earn
is based on, you know,

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:25.043
how much they like the show...
-Sure.

00:07:25.043 --> 00:07:27.012
-...and how successful
the show is

00:07:27.012 --> 00:07:29.448
and, you know, how much
they like your contribution

00:07:29.448 --> 00:07:30.683
to the show and everything.

00:07:30.683 --> 00:07:33.885
And I feel like the gift
that you get

00:07:33.885 --> 00:07:37.890
is how much they like you.
-Uh-huh. Yeah, yeah.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.124
-Wouldn't you think?
-I see. Right.

00:07:39.124 --> 00:07:40.893
So the salary is for the work.

00:07:40.893 --> 00:07:42.194
-For the work.
-The gift is personal.

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:43.862
-And then the gift is --
Yeah, because it's gratuitous.

00:07:43.862 --> 00:07:45.664
-This is James, the person.
-It's completely gratuitous.

00:07:45.664 --> 00:07:47.366
I don't need a gift really,
you know?

00:07:47.366 --> 00:07:49.835
-Yeah.
-It's a -- It's a gesture.

00:07:49.835 --> 00:07:52.938
It's how much we like you.

00:07:52.938 --> 00:07:56.909
My agents,
they always give me wine.

00:07:56.909 --> 00:07:59.144
-Yeah. Do you like wine?
-I love wine.

00:07:59.144 --> 00:08:01.814
-Perfect.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:01.814 --> 00:08:05.351
-So, this came in an Hermes bag.
-Okay.

00:08:05.351 --> 00:08:08.620
-And I thought, "Wow."
-Yeah.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.290
-"They really like me."
-Yeah.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.826
-This is an expensive store.

00:08:13.826 --> 00:08:15.927
And I took it out,

00:08:15.927 --> 00:08:18.864
and it was just

00:08:18.864 --> 00:08:21.000
a square of leather.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.101
[ Laughter ]

00:08:23.101 --> 00:08:24.603
-Uh-huh.

00:08:24.603 --> 00:08:26.606
-Which I've seen before,
you know?

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:28.106
I -- You know, like, when --

00:08:28.106 --> 00:08:29.842
We just did a renovation
at our house,

00:08:29.842 --> 00:08:33.346
and I was sent the same thing
as a sample

00:08:33.346 --> 00:08:35.247
to what we might want
to cover the couch with.

00:08:35.247 --> 00:08:37.083
-Okay. Gotcha.
-You know?

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:38.951
-So it was like --
it was like a leather sample?

00:08:38.951 --> 00:08:41.954
-It was like a leather sample.
It's Hermes.

00:08:41.954 --> 00:08:44.423
I thought maybe it's a sample
for what's possible

00:08:44.423 --> 00:08:45.824
to make a saddle out of.

00:08:45.824 --> 00:08:47.393
[ Laughter ]
-Okay.

00:08:47.393 --> 00:08:49.828
-I really didn't have any idea
what it was.

00:08:49.828 --> 00:08:51.130
-Gotcha.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:55.301
-So I called my friend Laura
who works upstairs,

00:08:55.301 --> 00:08:57.603
literally and figuratively.

00:08:57.603 --> 00:08:59.238
And so she came
to my dressing room,

00:08:59.238 --> 00:09:01.407
and I said,
"What the hell is this?"

00:09:01.407 --> 00:09:03.743
Because I was
about to go downstairs...

00:09:03.743 --> 00:09:05.076
-Yeah.

00:09:05.076 --> 00:09:07.646
-...to -- There were going
to be people there that --

00:09:07.646 --> 00:09:09.014
-That had given it to you.

00:09:09.014 --> 00:09:10.716
-Not really.
-Yeah.

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:12.385
[ Laughter ]

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:15.421
-Anyway, I said, "What is this?"

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:18.823
And she said, "Oh. Yes.

00:09:18.823 --> 00:09:20.793
I got the same thing.

00:09:20.793 --> 00:09:22.728
[ Laughter ]

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:24.530
It's a mouse pad."

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:27.366
[ Laughter ]

00:09:36.308 --> 00:09:39.779
I mean, I -- Truly, I didn't
even really know what she meant.

00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:41.313
[ Laughter ]

00:09:41.313 --> 00:09:43.248
I don't own a computer.

00:09:43.248 --> 00:09:44.717
-Right.

00:09:46.118 --> 00:09:47.686
-You know, I thought
it was the place

00:09:47.686 --> 00:09:50.723
where your domesticated mouse
goes to the bathroom.

00:09:50.723 --> 00:09:52.124
[ Laughter ]

00:09:52.124 --> 00:09:53.593
-I didn't know -- True story.

00:09:53.593 --> 00:09:56.061
Backstage in your dressing room,
I said,

00:09:56.061 --> 00:09:58.230
"Oh, I'll send you
a link to something."

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.198
And you looked at me --

00:10:00.198 --> 00:10:03.802
You gave me
a real Flaco the Owl look

00:10:03.802 --> 00:10:06.138
when I said "link."

00:10:06.138 --> 00:10:09.174
So I -- I can appreciate
that a mouse pad did not --

00:10:09.174 --> 00:10:10.676
was not straight to your heart.

00:10:10.676 --> 00:10:12.545
[ Laughter ]

00:10:12.545 --> 00:10:14.847
-I feel much better about Flaco.

00:10:14.847 --> 00:10:16.815
[ Laughter ]

00:10:16.815 --> 00:10:19.518
He could do a TV show.

00:10:19.518 --> 00:10:21.954
-James, you know my favorite
thing about having you here?

00:10:21.954 --> 00:10:23.689
-Yeah.
-Because our time is almost up,

00:10:23.689 --> 00:10:25.891
so I want to tell you my --
-It always is.

00:10:25.891 --> 00:10:28.060
[ Laughter ]
-I don't think you've ever --

00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:29.995
Of any guest I've ever had here,

00:10:29.995 --> 00:10:32.731
i don't think you've ever told
two stories.

00:10:32.731 --> 00:10:35.034
[ Laughter ]

00:10:37.435 --> 00:10:39.571
And you don't ever have to.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:39.571 --> 00:10:42.174
For my money,
you don't ever have to.

00:10:42.174 --> 00:10:44.844
The one, the only --
James Spader, everybody.

00:10:44.844 --> 00:10:46.778
200th episode of "The Blacklist"

00:10:46.778 --> 00:10:49.815
airs this Sunday, March 19th,
at 10:00 p.m. here on NBC.

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:51.784
We'll be right back
with Ian McShane.

